const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does shipping from China to Greece take?',
    a: 'Allow roughly 33–40 days port-to-port by sea from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to Piraeus via the Cape (MEDIUM confidence, planning estimate anchored to the ~33-day Genoa Mediterranean benchmark), plus the inland leg — versus roughly 22–28 days via Suez if the Red Sea reopens, because Piraeus is the first major EU call after the Suez Canal. No verified Greek air or rail transit is published, so request those from your carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does shipping from China to Greece cost?',
    a: 'The published Mediterranean corridor reference is $1,300–$5,200 for a 20ft, $1,800–$7,200 for a 40ft and $70–$170 per CBM for LCL (all MEDIUM confidence, Genoa reference) — Piraeus- and Thessaloniki-specific figures are not published and must be requested. On the FCL/LCL split the planning break-even sits near 15 CBM: below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in Greece?',
    a: 'Greece applies a 24% standard VAT rate, with reduced rates of 13% and 6% on some goods. Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so 5% duty plus 24% VAT compounds to ×1.302, not 29% — a $10,000 CIF shipment at 5% duty lands at $13,020, not the $12,900 a simple sum of the two rates would give.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to Greece?',
    a: 'Piraeus (GRPIR) is the deep-water default — Europe\u2019s only China-owned container hub (COSCO 67%) and the gateway sitting ~10 km from Athens — while Thessaloniki (GRSKG) serves northern Greece and the Western Balkans ~500 km above Athens. For Athens and the south, Piraeus wins on both proximity and direct-call frequency; for northern Greece or North Macedonia/Serbia cargo, Thessaloniki is the closer gate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is Piraeus owned by a Chinese company, and does it matter for shipping?',
    a: 'COSCO Shipping Ports (CSP), the terminal arm of China\u2019s state carrier COSCO, holds a 67% majority stake in Piraeus Port Authority (OLP) — acquired 51% in 2016 and raised to 67% in 2021. That makes Piraeus the only EU gateway run by a Chinese state carrier, which matters in two ways: COSCO\u2019s own Asia–Europe mainline services are structurally tied to Piraeus (more reliable direct calls than a neutral Med port), and Piraeus is the maritime terminus of the Belt-and-Road rather than a purely commercial Greek port.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does the Red Sea crisis change shipping times to Greece?',
    a: 'It inverts Piraeus\u2019s position more than any other EU gateway. On the Suez routing Piraeus is the first major EU call after the canal (~22–28 days); on the Cape routing ships go around Africa, through Gibraltar and across the whole Med, so Piraeus becomes one of the last calls (~33–40 days) — a swing of roughly 11–18 days. The first question on any Greece quote is therefore \u201Cwhich routing\u201D, not which carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does Greece\u2019s landed cost compare with Italy\u2019s and Sweden\u2019s?',
    a: 'On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty, Greece\u2019s 24% VAT lands at $13,020 versus Italy\u2019s 22% at $12,810 — a $210 gap, exactly 2 points on the duty-inclusive base — and versus Sweden\u2019s 25% at $13,125, Greece is actually $105 cheaper. Against Germany\u2019s 19% ($12,495), the same shipment is $525 higher in Greece.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI to import into Greece?',
    a: 'Yes — one EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU, and it must exist before the goods arrive; zero Greek imports clear without it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Greece?',
    a: 'FCL is priced per container (Mediterranean corridor benchmark 20ft $1,300–$5,200, 40ft $1,800–$7,200; Piraeus-specific figures are not published and must be requested) and LCL per cubic metre ($70–$170/CBM), with the planning break-even near 15 CBM — below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship from China to Greece by rail?',
    a: 'Not practically today. Greece is a maritime-Silk-Road market — its China connection runs through Piraeus, not a land bridge — and no mainstream China→Greece rail lane is published in the snapshot. The Budapest–Belgrade railway is the emerging BRI land link to the Piraeus corridor, but it is not yet a volume China-Europe freight lane, so rail stays a request-for-quote line rather than a default option.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Piraeus a gateway for the Balkans, not just Greece?',
    a: 'Yes — that is the reason COSCO invested. Piraeus feeds Athens (~10 km) but its real strategic value is as a sea anchor for the landlocked Western Balkans: North Macedonia, Serbia, Kosovo and Bulgaria, reached by rail/road from the port. On that basis Piraeus competes directly with Koper, Constanța and Hamburg for Balkans-bound cargo, not just with other Greek ports.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for Greek customs clearance?',
    a: 'The core set is four documents plus one classification — commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC code, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products add CE marking evidence and chemicals add REACH registration.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I defer VAT when importing into Greece?',
    a: 'Greece, like other EU states, allows EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) to defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU, worth more cash at 24% VAT than in a 19% or 20% market.',
  },
];

