  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Greece deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Greece breaks the two assumptions that importers bring from the northern range — and it does so for
      one structural reason no other EU member can claim. The country data shows <strong>Athens</strong> as
      the capital, the currency as <strong>EUR</strong>, and a <strong>24% standard VAT</strong> layered on
      top of the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong> — a duty line that is uniform across every member
      state and varies only by HS code, typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU
      free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first assumption to break is that Piraeus is "just another Mediterranean port". It is not — it is
      the <strong>only EU gateway owned by a Chinese state carrier</strong>. COSCO Shipping Ports holds a
      <strong>67% majority stake</strong> in Piraeus Port Authority (OLP), acquired 51% in 2016 and raised to
      67% in 2021. That ownership makes Piraeus the maritime terminus of the Belt-and-Road — a China-controlled
      distribution hub sitting <em>inside</em> the EU customs union — and it ties COSCO's own Asia–Europe
      mainline services to the port in a way a neutral Mediterranean gateway cannot match.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second assumption is that Greece is a rail-through market like Poland or Germany. It is not. Greece's
      China connection is fundamentally <strong>maritime</strong>: no mainstream China→Greece rail lane exists
      in the snapshot, and the Budapest–Belgrade railway — the emerging BRI land link to the Piraeus corridor —
      is not yet a volume freight lane. Greece is a <strong>maritime-Silk-Road country</strong>, which changes
      the sea-versus-rail-versus-air decision from the one a Central European importer faces.
    </p>
    <p>
      The third variable is geography as risk: Piraeus sits at the far eastern edge of the Mediterranean, so its
      transit advantage <strong>inverts</strong> with the Red Sea. On the Suez routing it is the first major EU
      call after the canal; on the Cape routing it is one of the last. No other gateway's position flips as hard,
      which makes "which routing" the first question on any Greek quote.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the commercial side the honest position mirrors Portugal and Sweden: <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are
      published only as a Genoa Mediterranean corridor reference</strong> (MEDIUM confidence), while Piraeus-,
      Thessaloniki-specific, rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not — so those lines stay explicit
      request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

