  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges that competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Greece's statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on CIF</strong>
        (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>24% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. Below
        that line sit the charges that are unique to Greece — the <strong>inland leg from Piraeus to
        Athens (~10 km, trivially short) or to Thessaloniki (~500 km)</strong> and the routing-sensitive
        <strong>sea leg that flips between Suez and Cape</strong> — plus the operational charges the verified
        snapshot does not quantify: THC, documentation, inspection, port storage, demurrage and detention. This
        is where a cheap freight quote becomes an expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.24.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf's 5%. For an illustrative
        USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500 duty-inclusive
        base, then 24% VAT of USD 2,520, giving USD 13,020 before inland and destination fees. The
        compound is <strong>1.05 × 1.24 = ×1.302</strong>, not the 29% you would get by simply adding
        the two headline rates. Rates and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current duty line
        and VAT treatment with the Independent Authority for Public Revenue (AADE) before relying on this
        example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The Genoa Mediterranean corridor reference, EU duty and 24% VAT are MEDIUM/HIGH confidence from route/customs sources. Unquantified Greek and port fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — landed cost & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">What most guides skip</h3>
      <p>
        Five lines change the Greek landed cost and rarely appear on a standard ocean quote:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Piraeus is the only EU gateway owned by a Chinese state carrier — use it.</strong>
          COSCO's 67% stake means its own Asia–Europe mainline services are structurally tied to Piraeus,
          giving it a direct-call reliability a neutral Med port does not have — yet most importers still
          default to a northern-range or western-Med port out of habit.</li>
        <li><strong>The Red Sea inverts Piraeus's position more than any other gateway.</strong> A Suez
          routing makes Piraeus the first major EU call (~22–28 days); a Cape routing makes it one of the
          last (~33–40 days). An 11–18-day swing is a budgeting variable, not a footnote.</li>
        <li><strong>Athens is ~10 km from Piraeus, but Thessaloniki is ~500 km.</strong> The inland leg is
          trivial for the capital market and significant for the north — so "shipping to Greece" splits into
          two different landed costs depending on which city the cargo actually serves.</li>
        <li><strong>Piraeus is a Balkans gateway, not just a Greek port.</strong> North Macedonia, Serbia,
          Kosovo and Bulgaria all feed through it, which means the port competes with Koper and Constanța on
          hinterland cost — a comparison a Greece-only quote never makes.</li>
        <li><strong>24% VAT is two points above Italy's 22%.</strong> On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty that is
          $210 more in landed tax than shipping into Italy, but still $105 less than Sweden's 25% — so the
          Greek tax position is middle-of-the-top-tier, not the ceiling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage vs detention: two clocks, two payees</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the charge the <strong>terminal</strong> applies when import cargo
        stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is the
        charge the <strong>ocean carrier</strong> (or rail operator) applies when the importer keeps the
        container beyond the equipment free time after collection. They sound similar, but they are
        separate contracts, separate free-time allowances and separate per-day rates — and in a
        transshipment-heavy route like Greece the detention clock can also run against a feeder carrier as
        well as the mainline line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Greek and Piraeus/Thessaloniki free-time periods and per-day amounts are not published in the
        verified snapshot, so do not assume a Rotterdam or Genoa schedule applies. Before booking, ask
        the carrier and the forwarder to put the Greek free time, the inland free time, demurrage rate
        and detention rate in writing. The most reliable avoidance tactic is documentary readiness:
        register the EORI early, lock the TARIC code, prepare the invoice/packing list/B-L and file the
        Greek declaration promptly so release happens before either free-time clock runs out.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Hidden charges to ask for on the quote</h3>
      <p>
        Request an itemised schedule that separates each destination line. A quote that only says "freight
        + customs" can hide <strong>THC</strong> at Piraeus/Thessaloniki, a <strong>documentation fee</strong>,
        a possible <strong>customs inspection fee</strong>, <strong>port storage</strong>, the
        <strong>inland Greek leg</strong>, any <strong>transshipment move</strong> on the routing, and the
        <strong>demurrage/detention</strong> exposure if release is delayed. Without those
        lines, a dollar-level freight quote cannot be compared or used for landed-cost budgeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: Greece duty, 24% VAT, EORI &amp; conformity</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      Greece applies the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, so duty is identical across member
      states and depends on the HS code — typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong>, with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force. Import <strong>24% VAT</strong> (13% and 6% reduced on
      some goods) is then charged on the <strong>duty-inclusive value</strong>, so the baseline statutory
      calculation is <code>(CIF + duty) × 1.24</code>. Specific goods can still carry different duty lines
      or VAT treatment, so the HS code remains the first compliance decision.
    </p>

    <h3>EORI — register before arrival</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>EORI number</strong> is mandatory for any import into the EU and must be registered
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for
      China-to-Greece cargo, so start the registration before the vessel departs, not while it is
      discharging at Piraeus or Thessaloniki.
    </p>

    <h3>TARIC classification and documents</h3>
    <p>
      Greece classifies goods under the <strong>10-digit TARIC code</strong>, which drives the duty
      line, VAT treatment, permits and restricted screening. The standard document set is a commercial
      invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct
      TARIC classification, backed by your EORI number. Confirm the current declaration workflow with a
      licensed Greek broker.
    </p>

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated
      products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others. <strong>REACH</strong> is
      the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Greece
      exactly as in every member state, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file
      before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>VAT deferral via EU customs warehousing</h3>
    <p>
      Greece, like other EU states, allows <strong>EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63)</strong> to
      defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for
      importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU. Given Greece's 24% VAT tier, the deferral
      is worth more cash than in a 19% or 20% market, so ask your broker about the procedure before
      assuming VAT must be paid at the quay.
    </p>

    <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
    <p>
      SABER and SASO are <strong>Saudi-only</strong> conformity systems and do not apply to Greece or the
      EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Greek quote. Greece uses CE marking and REACH
      instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with the Independent Authority for
      Public Revenue (AADE) or your broker.
    </p>

    <h3>De-minimis and IOSS</h3>
    <p>
      The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro
      on most consignments. The <strong>€150</strong> figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the
      <strong>IOSS</strong> scheme simplifies VAT on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the
      current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with the Greek customs authority
      before relying on these figures.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Greece customs, duty, VAT & conformity" />
  </section>

